/*
 * Architecture overview for lintel.
 *
 * This is a hand-written page, not a header: it is the entry point a new user
 * needs before the generated class reference makes sense. It is pulled into
 * the Doxygen run via INPUT in Doxyfile and therefore shows up under
 * "Related Pages" in the published HTML at https://dkruempe.github.io/lintel/.
 *
 * Style rules that apply here (WARN_AS_ERROR=YES, so a typo fails CI):
 *   - use \page / \section / \subsection with a single-word id,
 *   - every @ref target must name a symbol that Doxygen actually parsed,
 *   - code fences use ```cpp / ```xml / ```bash.
 */

/**
 * @page architecture Architecture
 *
 * @section arch_layers Layers
 *
 * `lintel` is split into two halves that are compiled into one library:
 *
 * - **`core`** - the engine. Process startup, dependency injection, lifecycle,
 *   configuration, persistence abstraction, logging and plugins. Nothing in
 *   `core` needs an optional transport or UI.
 * - **`features`** - opt-in modules that plug into the engine. Each feature
 *   registers its own types into the container and is enabled explicitly by
 *   the application:
 *
 * | Feature | Header | What it adds |
 * |---|---|---|
 * | `PropertyFeature` | `lintel/app/PropertyFeature.h` | configuration properties, repositories, change notification |
 * | `BaseFeature` | `lintel/app/BaseFeature.h` | user/groups/history services, shared memory, process control |
 * | `CommandLineFeature` | `lintel/app/CommandLineFeature.h` | interactive CLI, the reference client for the HTTP API |
 * | `HttpFeature` | `lintel/app/HttpFeature.h` | cpp-httplib server and client, the HTTP API |
 *
 * The split is both a source-level and a link-time boundary. Each layer is an
 * exported CMake target with a strictly acyclic dependency graph
 * (`core` &larr; `base` &larr; `property` &larr; `http` &larr; `cli` &larr; `app`),
 * so an application can link only the layers it needs (e.g. `lintel::property`).
 * The composition classes (`StartupBuilder`, `ConfigurationComponentBuilder` and
 * the `*Feature` types) live in `lintel::app`, the top layer. The historical
 * `lintel::lintel` aggregate target still links everything and remains the
 * default entry point.
 *
 * @section arch_bootstrap Bootstrap and lifecycle
 *
 * Every application starts through @ref lintel::StartupBuilder. `StartupBuilder::with(argc, argv)`
 * is the factory, `addFeature<FEATURE>()` accumulates the feature set and
 * `start()` runs the sequence. `start()` executes these phases in a fixed
 * order, and the order is the contract:
 *
 * 1. **Type registration.** `Configuration`, `ProcessName` and
 *    `EnvironmentConfiguration` are registered as container instances, then
 *    every feature's `registerTypes(builder, configuration, processName)` runs.
 *    Configuration is available at *registration* time on purpose: a feature
 *    can then register a type only in the process the configuration names as
 *    its owner (`BaseFeature` does exactly this for `HistoryService`).
 * 2. **Logger.** `DECLARE_LOGGER(m_name, m_configuration)` after registration,
 *    so log statements during later phases are routed.
 * 3. **Signal mask.** `SIGINT`, `SIGCHLD` and `SIGTERM` are blocked via
 *    `pthread_sigmask` and consumed later by a dedicated `sigwait` thread
 *    instead of `std::signal`, which avoids the async-signal-safety problems of
 *    handlers that touch C++ state.
 * 4. **Container build.** `builder.build()` instantiates the object graph.
 * 5. **Bootstrap plugins.** `BootstrapService::onStart()` runs them in
 *    `BootstrapSequence` order: `SingleInstance`, `Database`, `MessageQueue`,
 *    `VirtualGroups`, `SharedMemory`, `AdminUser`. The order is data
 *    dependencies, not priority preference - the admin user cannot exist
 *    before groups, and shared memory cannot be mirrored before the database
 *    is seeded.
 * 6. **Feature initialization.** Each feature's `initialize(container)` runs and
 *    binds services to the now-resolved graph.
 * 7. **Arguments.** `ProcessArgumentService::parseArguments()` consumes the
 *    command line, which is why it happens after initialization and not in
 *    phase 1.
 * 8. **Awake.** `PersistableService::awake()` tells every registered
 *    `PersistableBean` to reload its state from persistent storage.
 * 9. **Service initialization.** `InitializeService::onInitialize()` calls
 *    `onInitialize()` on every resolved @ref lintel::AbstractServiceInterface, in
 *    container registration order.
 * 10. **Idle.** The builder waits on a condition variable until the signal
 *     thread sets the stop flag, then joins it. The destructor joins as well,
 *     so a `REQUIRE` failure that unwinds through a step cannot leave a
 *     joinable `std::thread` behind and abort the process.
 *
 * @subsection arch_feature_deps Feature dependencies
 *
 * The link chain `core &rarr; base &rarr; property &rarr; http &rarr; cli &rarr; app` is a
 * compile-time guarantee (`PUBLIC` in `src/CMakeLists.txt`), but *activation* runs through
 * `addFeature<>()` and used to be unchecked: `addFeature<PropertyFeature>()` without
 * `addFeature<BaseFeature>()` started, and the DI auto-registration resolved a transient,
 * empty service object instead of failing - a silent degradation that surfaced far away
 * from its cause.
 *
 * A feature therefore declares the layers it needs with
 * `FeatureInterface::requiredFeatures()` (`PropertyFeature` &rarr; `Base`,
 * `HttpFeature` &rarr; `Property`, `CommandLineFeature` &rarr; `Http`), and
 * `StartupBuilder::verifyFeatureDependencies()` checks it **before** the types are
 * registered (before phase 1 above). Every required feature has to be present in the
 * feature vector *and* at a smaller index - the chain is linear, so a positional check
 * covers the topology without a graph search - otherwise `start()` throws with the
 * missing feature's name. Because the vector index also decides `initialize()` and
 * `resolveAll()` order, "declared" and "ordered correctly" are the same condition here.
 * The reference application in `examples/` shows the canonical order: `Base`, `Property`,
 * `Http`, `Cli`, `TestFeature`.
 *
 * @subsection arch_shutdown Graceful shutdown
 *
 * The `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` that arrives in the `sigwait` thread (or a Windows
 * console control event) calls `StartupBuilder::onShutdown()`. It sets the stop
 * flag **under the same mutex** that `start()` waits on, so the wakeup cannot be
 * lost, then sorts the resolved services by `shutdownPriorityOf()` **ascending**
 * and calls `onShutdown()` on each in that order: `ShutdownPriority::DEFAULT`
 * (0) services stop first, the `ShutdownPriority::PROCESS_SERVICE` (1)
 * `ProcessService` stops last. Keeping the process manager alive to the end lets
 * it keep reaping children (`SIGCHLD`) while the rest of the process winds down,
 * and only then detach from/terminate the managed processes. When every
 * `onShutdown()` has run, the condition variable is notified and `start()`
 * returns, joining the signal thread. The order is pinned by
 * `tests/core/StartupBuilderTest.cpp`.
 *
 * The HTTP server is deliberately *not* part of that sequence: it is created
 * eagerly by `ServerProvider` while the container is built (phase 4) and held by
 * the container, not by an `AbstractServiceInterface`. It therefore keeps
 * accepting and serving for the whole lifetime of the process and is only
 * stopped when the container - and with it `ServerProvider` - is destroyed
 * after `start()` returned. `Server::Impl`'s destructor calls `stop()` and joins
 * the listen thread, which lets an in-flight request finish instead of cutting
 * the connection. A deployment that wants to stop taking new work before the
 * services wind down has to do that at the infrastructure level (readiness below).
 *
 * @section arch_di Dependency injection
 *
 * The container is the library's own header-only `lintel::di`
 * (`src/include/lintel/core/di/Container.h`). It replaces the former vendored
 * Hypodermic, which was unmaintained since 2017. It is used through two
 * interface methods rather than exposed in the API:
 *
 * - `FeatureInterface::registerTypes()` / `FeatureInterface::initialize()`
 * - `BootstrapPlugin::onStart()` / `BootstrapPlugin::getPriority()`
 *
 * Public headers only mention `lintel::di::Container` / `ContainerBuilder`
 * through forward declarations where a signature needs it, so consumers are
 * not forced to have the container's header on their include path.
 *
 * Constructor parameters are injected when they are `std::shared_ptr<T>`
 * (resolved as the last registration of `T`) or `std::vector<std::shared_ptr<T>>`
 * (all registrations of `T`, in registration order). Unregistered concrete types
 * are auto-registered transiently, and a thread-local activation stack turns a
 * resumed registration (a dependency cycle) into a `ResolutionException` instead
 * of unbounded recursion.
 *
 * @section arch_properties Properties
 *
 * The property system is the central configuration surface: services read
 * their settings through it instead of parsing XML themselves.
 *
 * - `PropertyRepository` is the storage abstraction, implemented by
 *    `FilePropertyRepository`, `DatabasePropertyRepository` and
 *    `SharedMemoryPropertyRepository`.
 * - Repositories form a *shadow chain*: a repository can declare itself a
 *    shadow of another, which is how a shared-memory mirror stays in sync with
 *    the database without either side knowing about the other's storage
 *    details. This is why `SharedMemoryPropertyRepository` can serve processes
 *    that must not touch a database at all.
 * - `PropertyFactory::Create()` materializes a `PropertyBase` from its string
 *    representation, using the recorded type name.
 * - Changes are published on the event bus, so a subscriber learns about a
 *   change without polling the repository.
 *
 * The bootstrap XML is expanded before parsing: every `${NAME}` occurrence whose
 * `NAME` matches `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` is replaced by the environment variable
 * of that name via `EnvironmentConfiguration::expand()`. An **unset** variable is
 * left as the literal text `${NAME}` (it is not an error), and `$${NAME}` escapes
 * to the literal `${NAME}`. A malformed XML document aborts startup with a
 * `ConfigurationException` that carries the tinyxml2 error and line number.
 *
 * @section arch_persistence Persistence
 *
 * `db::Connection` is one class over both backends; the concrete choice is
 * `db::ConnectionType` plus a connection entry from the bootstrap XML. Around
 * it sit `db::Parameter` / `db::ParameterBuilder` / `db::PreparedStatement`
 * (bind parameters), `db::Result`, `db::Cursor` (server-side cursors for
 * PostgreSQL), `db::Transaction` and `db::Notify`.
 *
 * Values are bound as text through the generic bind, which means a caller is
 * responsible for not passing plaintext secrets. Both password paths in the
 * library (`Cryption::hashOf` on login and on change) hash before the value
 * reaches this layer.
 *
 * One limit is deliberate and documented instead of worked around: neither
 * backend lets the library scrub a decrypted secret from its own memory. The
 * drivers copy values into buffers the application cannot reach afterwards, so
 * a "secure erase" of the plaintext would only clear a copy. What is kept short
 * instead is the exposure - passwords are hashed before they reach this layer,
 * sessions live in memory rather than in a session table, and the SQLite file
 * is created under the user's home directory. A deployment that needs secrets
 * scrubbed from memory needs a backend that never returns them to the client.
 *
 * @subsection arch_persistence_pool Connection pooling
 *
 * `db::ConnectionPool` opens a connection once per slot and hands it out through
 * a movable, non-copyable `db::ConnectionPool::Lease` (RAII: the connection
 * returns to the pool when the lease leaves scope, also while an exception
 * unwinds). `Lease::connection()` exposes the `db::Connection &`. One
 * implementation covers three operating modes, selected by the `poolSize`
 * attribute of a `DatabaseConnection`:
 *
 * | `poolSize` | behaviour |
 * |---|---|
 * | `0` | pooling disabled - the historical connection per call, and **no** pool is registered |
 * | `1` | one persistent, serialized connection (the SQLite default) |
 * | `N>1` | bounded pool for parallel callers (the PostgreSQL default is 4) |
 *
 * The pool is thread safe (mutex plus condition variable). An `acquire()` waits
 * at most `poolAcquireTimeoutMs` (default 5000); on expiry it throws
 * `db::PoolTimeoutException` (a `db::SQLException`), which names the pool and its
 * maximum and lists every outstanding lease with its `std::source_location`,
 * thread id and age. A thread that already holds a lease of the same pool and
 * acquires again fails **immediately** instead of waiting: the re-entrancy
 * detector turns the typical `poolSize=1` nested-call deadlock into a fast,
 * named error. On release the connection is probed by `db::Connection::isHealthy`
 * (`PQstatus` for PostgreSQL, a `SELECT 1` probe for SQLite) and a broken
 * connection is dropped, so the next acquire builds a fresh one.
 *
 * Pools are reachable by connection name through `db::ConnectionPoolRegistry`, a
 * process-wide singleton (the `ReadinessRegistry` pattern - the DI container
 * resolves by type, not by name, so several pools could not be addressed).
 * `DatabaseBootstrapPlugin::onStart()` registers a pool for every configured
 * entry with `poolSize > 0`. The registry never creates a pool implicitly: a
 * process without database entries (the worker's `bootstrap_worker`) has an empty
 * registry and `poolOf()` throws `db::PoolNotFoundException`. The
 * `database:<name>` readiness check acquires a lease and runs `SELECT 1` on it,
 * so it also exercises the pool health path; a connection without a pool keeps
 * the direct probe.
 *
 * **Session state does not survive a release.** A reused connection keeps
 * `SET`, temporary tables and prepared statements, so code that runs on a lease
 * must not rely on connection-local state surviving a pool release. The
 * PostgreSQL `postgresql::PreparedStatement` deallocates its named statement in
 * its destructor for exactly this reason; prefer unnamed, per-call statements
 * for anything that must not leak. A SQLite `poolSize > 1` is rejected at parse
 * time (`ConfigurationException`) unless
 * `allowMultipleSqliteConnections="true"` overrides it, because the file lock
 * and WAL make parallel writers contend. A pool is not a replacement for an
 * external pooler (PgBouncer/pgcat); it is a documented, optional complement.
 *
 * **ScopedConnection & lease lifetime:** All direct connection constructions
 * are replaced by `db::ScopedConnection`. If `ConnectionPoolRegistry` has a
 * pool for the entry's name, it acquires a `db::ConnectionPool::Lease`; otherwise
 * it opens a direct `db::Connection` (fallback when `poolSize == 0` or no pool
 * configured). The lease lives as long as the `ScopedConnection`.
 *
 * **Transactions:** `db::Transaction` must run within the `ScopedConnection`
 * scope that supplies the connection. The connection must not return to the pool
 * before commit or rollback; keep `ScopedConnection` alive for the entire
 * transaction.
 *
 * **Cursors:** `db::Cursor` references `db::Connection &`, so the
 * `ScopedConnection` that provides it must outlive the cursor. Repository
 * cursors are method-local, which matches creating `ScopedConnection` in the
 * same scope.
 *
 * **Bootstrap migrations:** Schema migrations (`initDatabase`/`executeInitFile`)
 * keep a single connection over the entire `*_version_N.sql` run; when pooled,
 * the holding `ScopedConnection` must span that loop.
 *
 * @section arch_ipc Shared memory and the event bus
 *
 * Shared memory is `boost::interprocess` based and deliberately quarantined:
 * `BoostSegmentAllocator` is the single adapter where `ShmSegmentAccessor`'s
 * allocator interface meets boost, so the rest of the library - and all
 * examples except the ones that create segments - stay boost-free. On top of
 * it, `EventBus` provides broadcast to subscribers plus point-to-point events
 * between processes.
 *
 * Cross-process access to a remapped segment is serialized by
 * `SegmentSemaphoreGuard`, which acquires and releases the segment's named
 * remap semaphore. The acquisition is bounded (`SegmentSemaphoreGuard::kDefaultWaitTimeout`
 * seconds): a process that crashes while holding the semaphore leaves its count at
 * zero, and an unbounded wait would stall every other process. On timeout the guard
 * throws instead of hanging.
 *
 * A bounded wait only moves the problem, so the orphaned lock is reclaimed as well -
 * but only when the holder is provably gone. Reclaiming the lock of a *live* process
 * would give two processes independent mutexes for one critical section, and a stuck
 * semaphore alone carries no information about its holder. Each segment therefore keeps
 * a `SegmentLockOwner` sidecar (`<name>_remap_own`, same suffix length as
 * `_remap_sem` because macOS caps named object names at 31 characters): the guard
 * records the holder's process id **and its start ticks** while it holds the lock and
 * clears it again before posting (`clearIf`, so a release cannot wipe a record that
 * already belongs to the next holder). The start ticks are what distinguishes a live
 * holder from a recycled process id; if they cannot be determined, the id alone decides
 * and the error direction is always "leave the lock alone". `shm::recoverOrphanedSegmentLock()`
 * then closes the reachable routes to a double lock:
 *
 * - It serializes itself with `boost::interprocess::file_lock` on the segment file, with a
 *   timeout (10 s) that makes a stalled holder give up instead of blocking the open path
 *   forever. Two processes that both found a dead holder could otherwise each remove the
 *   semaphore the other had just created and keep a lock of their own. A file lock is
 *   released by the kernel when its holder dies, so this mutex cannot become an orphan.
 * - It probes the semaphore with a non-blocking acquire: a free lock has nothing to
 *   recover, and recreating it would orphan every open handle.
 * - A taken lock is re-checked after `shm::kRecoveryProbeDelay` against the holder record,
 *   freshly re-read from the shared cells: a crashed holder that recorded itself is
 *   reclaimed, a live holder - whether it was recorded before or took the lock over during
 *   the wait - is left alone. The remaining window is the acquisition and the recording of
 *   it, which only a holder that is stalled for longer than the probe delay can expose; the
 *   timeout path of the waiters stays the fallback for that.
 *
 * A reclaimed lock lets every waiter continue; a process that already opened the removed
 * semaphore still talks to the orphaned one and times out with the usual error instead
 * of racing. The recovery runs once when a segment is opened (`SharedMemoryService::create()`)
 * and again when `ISharedMemoryService::recover()` is called for segments that are already
 * mapped; it never makes a manual decision - if uncertain, it leaves the lock alone and
 * the waiters time out. `examples/cleanup.cpp` remains the manual path when the automatic
 * recovery cannot decide: given a segment name it removes the remap semaphore and the
 * `SegmentLockOwner` sidecar (plus the named mutex of that name). **Message queues** have no
 * holder record at all and are therefore never reclaimed automatically - removing them is
 * always a manual decision.
 * Covered by `tests/features/SharedMemoryServiceTest.cpp` (dead holder is reclaimed,
 * a free lock with a stale record is left alone, an unrecorded stuck lock is reclaimed,
 * a living holder is left alone, a release cannot wipe the next holder's record,
 * recovery while opening).
 *
 * Sizing semantics of @ref lintel::SharedMemoryService:
 *
 * - A segment is created with its configured initial size. With an
 *   `AutoExtendConfig` it also has a grow increment and a hard `maxSize`.
 * - `growOf()` grows by the requested amount. A `grow` of zero is a no-op.
 *   With `maxSize != 0` the request is capped to the remaining headroom, and
 *   growing a segment that is already at `maxSize` throws. With `maxSize == 0`
 *   the segment is unbounded (the historical behavior).
 * - `shrinkOf()` shrinks the file to the size its current contents need.
 * - Both operations reopen the mapping (the `mmap` length is fixed at mapping
 *   time) and bump the generation counter, so repositories notice that
 *   references into the old mapping are invalid and re-fetch them.
 * - Unknown segment names raise @ref lintel::ShmSegmentNotFound.
 *
 * @section arch_transports Transports
 *
 * The HTTP API (@ref lintel::HttpFeature) and the CLI (@ref lintel::CommandLineFeature) are
 * both optional, and the CLI is deliberately the reference client for the HTTP
 * API. The wire contract is `swagger.yaml` in the repository root; `swagger.md`
 * is a generated mirror of it. Server behavior worth knowing:
 *
 * - `/health` is unauthenticated and **always** answers `200` with
 *    `{"status":"ok"}`: it is a liveness probe that must not consult any
 *    dependency, otherwise a broken database would make the supervisor restart a
 *    healthy process.
 * - `/ready` is the readiness probe. It runs every check registered in
 *    @ref lintel::ReadinessRegistry - a process-wide registry that components
 *    owning a dependency fill during startup (`DatabaseBootstrapPlugin` registers
 *    one `database:<name>` check per configured connection, `SharedMemoryService`
 *    a `shared_memory` check). With no check registered it answers the historical
 *    `200 {"status":"ready"}`; otherwise it answers `200` with
 *    `{"status":"ready","checks":{"<name>":"ok",...}}` when every check passed,
 *    or `503` with `{"status":"not_ready","checks":{"<name>":"failed: <reason>",...}}`.
 *    A check that throws counts as failed; the body is written with rapidjson, so
 *    a reason taken from an exception message is escaped and cannot break the
 *    document. A check reconnects/pings its dependency per request, which is what
 *    a load balancer or Kubernetes probe wants: it takes the process out of
 *    rotation while a dependency is down.
 * - TLS is configured per `<HttpHost><Server>` in the bootstrap XML. With
 *    `require_tls="true"` the server refuses to start without a certificate and
 *    key, which keeps credentials from being served in clear text. A certificate
 *    that OpenSSL cannot load is reported as such at startup instead of surfacing
 *    later as a failed bind.
 * - A renewed certificate is applied **without a restart** by
 *    `POST /admin/reload-tls` (admin group). The running server registers a
 *    handler in @ref lintel::TlsReloadRegistry and the endpoint runs it,
 *    answering `200` with `{"status":"reloaded","results":{"tls":"ok"}}`, `500`
 *    with `"failed: <reason>"` when a file cannot be read or the pair is
 *    rejected, and `409 {"status":"nothing_to_reload"}` when the process serves
 *    clear text. The certificate pair is swapped under the context mutex the
 *    handshake path also takes, so a handshake starting at the same time sees
 *    either the old or the new certificate. The server keeps the last pair that
 *    worked and puts it back when a renewal is rejected - the loader installs the
 *    certificate before it validates the key, so a mismatched pair would
 *    otherwise leave the running server unable to complete a handshake.
 * - **Request correlation.** Every response carries an `X-Request-Id` header:
 *    the value the client sent is echoed when it matches `[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,64}`,
 *    otherwise the server generates one - the id is written to the log
 *    unescaped, so an unchecked header could break the JSON format
 *    (`ADD_HANDLER_METHOD`/`ADD_HANDLER_CONTENT_READER_METHOD` in
 *    @ref lintel::Controller install it for every handler). The same id is pushed
 *    onto the handling thread as a `RequestIdGuard`, so the log lines produced
 *    while the request is handled carry it as a prefix (`[<id>] message`) and a
 *    single search of one process's log returns everything that belongs to one
 *    request - on both sides of the wire. The thread-local id is restored when
 *    the request finishes, so a pooled worker thread cannot tag a later request
 *    with an earlier correlation id.
 * - **Log level of a running process:** `POST /admin/log-level` (admin group
 *    `Admin-Log`) applies `{"level":"<name>"}` to every subsequent log call,
 *    answering `200 {"status":"changed","level":"debug"}`, `400
 *    {"status":"invalid_level"}` for an unknown name or a body that is not json,
 *    `401` outside the group and `403` for a wrong content type. The bootstrap
 *    XML is *not* rewritten: a restart falls back to the level the operator
 *    deployed, which keeps a temporary diagnosis from becoming the new default.
 *    The level itself lives in @ref lintel::LoggerService (`setLevel()`/
 *    `levelOf()`), so the same capability exists in-process; `<Logger json="true">`
 *    switches the formatting to one JSON object per line for a log shipper.
 * - `trusted_proxies` lists the proxies whose `X-Forwarded-For` header is
 *    believed; without it the header is ignored and the peer address is used.
 *    That list decides which address a token is bound to and which address the
 *    login rate limit and lockout are keyed by, so it has to name exactly the
 *    proxies in front of the process: a range that is too wide lets a client
 *    pick its own `X-Forwarded-For` and rotate it per request, which turns the
 *    per-address brute-force protection into no protection at all.
 * - Request latency is a transport property, not an afterthought. The server
 *    calls `set_tcp_nodelay(true)`: httplib leaves Nagle enabled
 *    (`CPPHTTPLIB_TCP_NODELAY` defaults to `false`) and a TLS response then waits
 *    for the peer's delayed acknowledgement, which cost ~40 ms per request and
 *    dominated every response the API sent. `benchmarks/HttpBenchmark.cpp` and
 *    `benchmarks/baseline.json` (checked by `scripts/check-benchmarks.sh`) pin
 *    that: reverting the option shows up as a >20x regression in
 *    `BM_Http_Health_TlsKeepAlive`. Nagle never delays a full segment, so the
 *    option costs nothing for bulk responses. Keep-alive is httplib's default and
 *    the client used by @ref lintel::Client sets the option for the same reason.
 *    TLS session resumption is already effective (OpenSSL 1.3 session tickets,
 *    verified with `openssl s_client -sess_out`/`-sess_in`), and the request body
 *    is capped at 8 MiB by `set_payload_max_length`, which rejects larger
 *    uploads instead of buffering them.
 *
 * @section arch_authentication Authentication and sessions
 *
 * @ref lintel::AuthService keeps the sessions in memory: a successful login mints
 * a token that carries the canonical client address, and an access is rejected
 * unless it comes from that same address. The token slides forward on every
 * access and expires after `m_timeoutLogin` (5 minutes) without one; the sweep
 * that removes them runs every `m_scheduleRate` seconds.
 *
 * Login is protected against brute force, keyed by the canonical client address
 * and measured in sliding windows:
 *
 * - `m_maxLoginAttempts` (20) attempts per `m_rateLimitWindow` (60 s); the next
 *   one is rejected.
 * - `m_maxLoginFailures` (5) failures inside `m_loginLockout` (60 s) lock the
 *   address out until the window passes; a successful login clears the failures
 *   recorded for that address.
 * - A rejected login answers exactly like a wrong password, so a caller cannot
 *   tell a locked-out address, an unknown user and a wrong password apart from
 *   the response; the reason only goes to the log.
 *
 * Three consequences of the per-address keying are worth planning for:
 *
 * - Clients that share one address (NAT, or a proxy missing from
 *   `trusted_proxies`) share one budget, so too many failed logins behind a NAT
 *   lock out everybody behind it.
 * - The address is canonicalized before it is stored, so one client in two
 *   spellings (`2001:DB8::1` and `2001:db8::1`, or an ipv4 client that an ipv6
 *   listener reports in its ipv4-mapped form) is one budget and one session,
 *   not two. This also means a session cannot be logged out from a differently
 *   spelled address.
 * - A client that opens many source addresses grows the per-address tables until
 *   the sweep prunes them at the end of the window; the tables are pruned per
 *   window, not per entry count.
 *
 * Passwords are stored as salted hashes (@ref lintel::Cryption). A legacy
 * unsalted SHA-512 hash is upgraded transparently on the next successful login.
 * Verification is skipped when the user does not exist, which is faster than
 * verifying a wrong password - the response is identical either way, only the
 * log entry and the timing differ.
 *
 * @section arch_extending Extension points
 *
 * The intended extension points, in the order a new subsystem usually needs
 * them:
 *
 * 1. **A service** derives from `AbstractService` (which supplies the
 *    `AbstractServiceInterface` lifecycle and property access) and registers
 *    itself in a feature's `registerTypes()`.
 * 2. **A bootstrap plugin** derives from @ref lintel::BootstrapPlugin and returns a
 *    `BootstrapSequence` value, so it lands at the right point in phase 5.
 * 3. **A new storage backend** implements `PropertyRepository`, or a
 *    `db::ConnectionType` case for the persistence layer.
 * 4. **A new transport** implements `FeatureInterface` and registers its own
 *    components; nothing in `core` has to change.
 *
 * @section arch_build Build and integration
 *
 * Consumers use the installed CMake package:
 *
 * ```cmake
 * find_package(lintel REQUIRED)
 * target_link_libraries(my_app PRIVATE lintel::lintel)
 * ```
 *
 * The package is relocatable - `INSTALL_INTERFACE` carries no build-machine
 * absolute paths - so an installed tree can be moved. Dependencies come from
 * Conan 2; `CONAN_SYSREQUIRES_MODE` is what lets `conan install` satisfy
 * system requirements during CI.
 *
 * Platform support in practice: the code is written against POSIX and the POSIX
 * details (`sigwait`, `localtime_r`, named shared memory, FIFOs) are isolated
 * behind `lintel/core/platform/Platform.h` and a handful of `#if defined(_WIN32)`
 * branches. The blocking CI matrix covers GCC (Release, gate) and Clang with
 * libstdc++ 14, plus one Debug/non-unity run, all on Ubuntu 24.04. The `macos`
 * and `windows` jobs are **advisory**: they build and run the tests, but are not
 * required checks and have no PostgreSQL coverage (see `ROADMAP.md` §8).
 */

/**
 * @page architecture_conventions Conventions
 *
 * Conventions worth knowing before reading the headers or contributing.
 *
 * @section conv_headers Header hygiene
 *
 * Public headers under `src/include/lintel/` are the supported surface. The
 * build is deliberately expensive to get wrong, so the rules are enforced:
 *
 * - `spdlog` does not appear in public headers. `LoggerService.h` forward
 *   declares `fmt` types instead, which removed the spdlog dependency from
 *   the include graph entirely.
 * - Boost is limited to the shared-memory boundary described in
 *   @ref architecture ("Quarantined boost").
 * - `cpp-httplib` is confined to the HTTP feature; controllers depend on an
 *   internal interface, and the implementation sits behind a pimpl so the
 *   server type does not leak into public headers.
 * - Forward declarations are preferred over includes for types that only
 *   appear in a signature.
 *
 * The `gcc-debug-nonunity` CI entry exists specifically to protect these rules:
 * a unity build silently supplies includes that a single translation unit
 * would not have, so building non-unity in Debug is what actually proves header
 * completeness.
 *
 * @section conv_namespaces Namespaces
 *
 * All public classes and free functions live in the `lintel` namespace, with
 * the persistence layer nested one level deeper in `lintel::db`, so a consumer
 * with its own `Property` or `Result` class is not forced to namespace-qualify
 * deliberately. The CMake export namespace is `lintel::lintel` and the include
 * guard of a public header is `LINTEL_...`, so the library name appears in all
 * three places. This was a deliberate breaking change; the CHANGELOG migration
 * table lists the former global names next to their `lintel::` counterparts.
 *
 * @section conv_errors Errors and degradation
 *
 * The library is exception-based; there is no `std::expected`/`Result` error
 * channel. The decision of *who* propagates and who degrades is made per layer
 * and is what keeps the layering (`core` &larr; `base` &larr; `property` &larr;
 * `http` &larr; `cli`) honest:
 *
 * | Layer | Contract |
 * |---|---|
 * | `core` persistence | throws `db::SQLException`, whose message carries the driver's own error text; never returns a partial result silently. |
 * | `core` configuration | throws `ConfigurationException` carrying the tinyxml2 error text and the offending line number. |
 * | `core` models / `core` shm | no I/O, therefore no error channel. |
 * | feature repositories | **degrade deliberately**: a persistence failure is logged (`LOG_ERROR`) and surfaced as an empty list or a no-op, because the repository is an override layer over the configured defaults - an empty result means "keep the default", not "data lost". Domain-validation failures, in contrast, do throw (`std::runtime_error`, `HttpBadRequestException`). |
 * | feature services | propagate domain exceptions; where a change is already effective in memory they log and continue instead of turning a local success into an HTTP 500. |
 * | `features/http` | maps internal exceptions to status codes (`HttpBadRequestException` &rarr; 400, unauthorized &rarr; 401) and catches at the handler boundary so a stray exception cannot become a dropped connection. |
 *
 * The rule of thumb: **a layer that can substitute a sensible default degrades
 * and logs; a layer that cannot must propagate.** `DatabasePropertyRepository`
 * is the reference implementation of the "degrade" case and spells out its
 * reasoning at the top of the file.
 *
 * Two further conventions:
 *
 * - A `switch` over an enum is required to have a `default` branch that either
 *   handles the case or throws. A fall-through out of a non-`void` function is
 *   undefined behaviour, and CodeQL flagged exactly that once in this codebase.
 * - Thread-unsafe C functions (`std::localtime`, `std::ctime`) are not used;
 *   the `_r` variants write into caller-owned storage.
 *
 * @section conv_tests Tests
 *
 * The suite is Catch2 plus trompeloeil, built as the single `base_tests`
 * binary. Two constraints that are easy to trip over:
 *
 * - New test files must be added to the `add_executable(base_tests ...)` list
 *   in `tests/CMakeLists.txt`. There is no globbing, so a new file is
 *   invisible until it is listed.
 * - `ctest --label-regex unit` runs each test case as its own process (via
 *   `catch_discover_tests`), which hides cross-test environment leakage that a
 *   single-process run of `./bin/base_tests` does expose. Both are worth
 *   running.
 */